WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:09.780
The ZOS system libraries are where a lot of the customization data for the operating system lives.

2
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:16.780
You'll find a lot of important information here, including where to find other important information, so kind of important.

3
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.280
We've mentioned a few of these before during the module on IPLing a system,

4
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.980
and I know you wanted to know more information about those datasets, so here we are, let's get started.

5
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.380
First up, sys1.linklib.

6
00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:36.780
This contains much of the executable code, also known as modules for ZOS components and utilities.

7
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:47.480
In here, you'll find the module MSTJCL00, which contains the initial JCL statements that start the master scheduler subsystem.

8
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.680
So that's how JES gets started.

9
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.080
sys1.LPAlib.

10
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:55.980
LPAlib stands for Link Pack Area Library.

11
00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.780
These modules are shared by all address spaces in the system,

12
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:07.380
and because they are read-only and not self-modifying, they can be used by a number of address spaces at the same time.

13
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:14.180
There are four types of modules you'll find in the LPA, dynamic, fixed, modified, pageable,

14
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:18.080
and it all comes down to how they should be handled in memory.

15
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.980
sys1.Nucleus contains the basic supervisor modules for the system,

16
00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:28.880
the core of the system that is required for it to begin loading the full version of the operating system.

17
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:32.180
We just recently talked about sys1.PROClib,

18
00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:38.180
which is where the IBM-supplied JCL procedures for performing system functions live.

19
00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:45.680
And my favorite, sys1.PARMlib contains the control parameters for ZOS and some program products.

20
00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.780
If you've used Linux or Unix-based operating system before, it's similar to what you'd find in slash etc.

21
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:58.580
So if you're looking to increase a value from 25 to 30 or change the name of what something on the system gets called,

22
00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:02.880
chances are it's in sys1.PARMlib.

23
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.880
What makes up a particular system is how these system libraries are configured.

24
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.080
In addition, there's also information that comes from other non-IBM vendors,

25
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:16.480
as well as how a particular company chooses to set up their system.

26
00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.680
You can organize a lot of this customization information into these six areas.

27
00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.680
There's the ZOS software, the non-ZOS software, middleware like Kix DB2,

28
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:34.180
non-IBM software that comes from other vendors, customization data, user-defined exits,

29
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:40.680
which boils down to ways that users have customized and extended software, as well as user data.

30
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.480
When a program calls a module, the system needs to know where it is in order to run it,

31
00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.680
and it has a certain order it goes through when searching for modules.

32
00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.580
The first place it looks is in the Job Pack area, or JPA.

33
00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:57.980
If it's not in there, it'll look in the Task Live.

34
00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:01.680
Next stop is the Step Live or Job Live.

35
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:10.180
These are specific DD names that can be used to allocate data sets to be searched ahead of the default system search order for programs.

36
00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:19.480
We then hit LPA, that Linked Pack area, where it will hit the dynamic, then the fixed, and then the modified, and then the pageable modules.

37
00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:26.180
And then Linked List, as specified in ProgXX and Linked List XX.

38
00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.480
By default, the Linked List begins with sys1.linklib, and there's more to follow,

39
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:37.880
but you can view and change the order in your ProgXX, and add other libraries to that list.

40
00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:44.180
By the way, the list of places that the system searches for modules is called a concatenation.

41
00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.080
In order for modules to work, they need to be ready to go.

42
00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.380
It's not practical to keep all modules loaded in main memory all the time,

43
00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:59.580
but each time the system has to go and look for a module, that takes time, and it pulls the CPU away from doing other important stuff.

44
00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.780
So there's this concept of a Library Lookaside Facility, or LLA.

45
00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:10.780
This reduces the amount of IO needed to locate and fetch modules from DASD storage.

46
00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:17.080
LLA takes a look at the modules and determines which ones could really benefit from being staged.

47
00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:24.580
Similarly, there's a VLF, or Virtual Lookaside Facility, which is used to store named objects in virtual storage

48
00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:29.580
and retrieve those on behalf of users in multiple address spaces.

49
00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.880
So let's look at how these get set up in a few examples.

50
00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:39.480
Let's start with the LoadXX member, which is one of the first modules that gets loaded at IPL time.

51
00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.680
In here, you'll see the parmlibe concatenation.

52
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.580
You can specify up to 16 partition data sets in here.

53
00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.680
You can see in this configuration, there is a sys0.IPLPARM,

54
00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:58.480
and it will automatically add sys1.parmlibe to the end of the concatenation, even if you don't specify it.

55
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.880
There's also the IEA sysxx member.

56
00:05:01.880 --> 00:05:04.880
Now, this is where the installation defaults go.

57
00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.180
These are the parameters that will not change unless you're altering the physical

58
00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.780
or logically defined environment that your system is operating in.

59
00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:17.180
If you want to see the concatenation without digging through the data sets,

60
00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:21.780
you can type dparmlibe, and it will list them out for you.

61
00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:26.480
You can also look at the IPL information with dIPL info.

62
00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.380
That will show you when the system was last IPLed, what the version of the operating system is,

63
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.280
and what the contents of IEA sys and IEA sim are.

64
00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.680
That determines which members get loaded.

65
00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.180
You'll also see the IODF device number and the IPL device and serial number.

66
00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:45.280
This command is critically important.

67
00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:51.480
I can remember from my sysprog days, sometimes we'd bring up a system and it would just not be behaving correctly.

68
00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.280
Something we thought we installed wasn't there, modules weren't loading.

69
00:05:55.280 --> 00:06:02.280
Step one is to issue a dIPL info and make sure that what you're running is what you think you're running.

70
00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:07.180
Maybe we forgot to change the IPL volume or change one of those IEA members.

71
00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.980
This is your sanity check right here.

72
00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:13.180
Each IEA member has its own purpose and story,

73
00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:18.680
and importantly, sections of documentation describing exactly how to use it.

74
00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.280
We're not going to go through them all here.

75
00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:26.580
The important thing to take away is where to look to find them, generally in the ZOS system libraries,

76
00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:31.080
and where to find more about them by using the manuals or a search engine.

77
00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:38.080
I do want to show you those IEA simxx members though, since we've covered the topic of symbols a little while ago,

78
00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:39.780
and I want to tie back to that.

79
00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:45.980
If you look in IEA sysxx, you'll see some of those static system symbols getting set,

80
00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:49.780
like sysname, which is equal to SC04.

81
00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.580
You can see a few other symbols getting set in there as well.

82
00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:57.980
So later on, when you want to reference a specific data set built specifically for that system,

83
00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:02.680
we can just say ampersand sysname, and we've used a symbol.

84
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:07.880
If you ever wanted to display the list of symbols available, just type dsymbols.

85
00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:13.080
And now that symbols doesn't sound like a real word anymore, I think it's time to move on to the next topic.
